<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>RoslynMsBuildSpike</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>RoslynMsBuildSpike</RootNamespace>
    <!--
      MSBuildLocator requires that the MSBuild assemblies NOT be resolved from this project's own
      output. Copying them here is the documented way to get an unloadable host.
    -->
    <ExcludeAssetsFromMSBuild>true</ExcludeAssetsFromMSBuild>

    <!--
      SCOPED TO THIS SPIKE ONLY, and it must not travel to the shipped extractor.

      A transitive vulnerability scan reports TEN high-severity advisories
      reachable from Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces.MSBuild 4.14.0 — GHSA-w3q9-fxm7-j8fq against
      the MSBuild packages, and eight against System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 9.0.0. Bumping
      Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core from the 17.7.2 it resolves by default to 17.14.8 clears the
      original advisory and lands on a newer one, which says something about the chain.

      Why the suppression is defensible HERE and nowhere else: every reference above carries
      ExcludeAssets="runtime", so none of these assemblies reach the output directory. At runtime
      MSBuild is loaded from the installed SDK by MSBuildLocator. These are compile-time reference
      assemblies for a spike that is never shipped or deployed.

      Why it is NOT defensible for the extractor: the shipped component will make the same choice of
      dependency, and P1-SUPPLY's licence and transitive-CVE gates apply to it in full. This is
      recorded as a Phase-2 finding in RESULT.md for the Security & Identity Architect, not resolved
      by this line.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1903</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The fixture lives under this directory so the spike is one self-contained folder, which means
      the default glob would otherwise compile the target repository's sources INTO the host. The
      fixture is input data to be loaded at runtime, never part of this build.
    -->
    <Compile Remove="fixture/**" />
    <None Remove="fixture/**" />
    <EmbeddedResource Remove="fixture/**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build.Locator" />
    <!--
      NOT ExcludeAssets="runtime". These are Roslyn, and the host needs them at runtime; only the
      MSBuild assemblies below must be withheld so MSBuildLocator can bind them from the SDK. An
      earlier revision excluded both and failed at the first workspace call with a
      FileNotFoundException for Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces — the distinction is load-bearing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces.MSBuild" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" />
    <!--
      Required, and its absence fails in a way worth remembering: without the C# LANGUAGE SERVICE,
      MSBuildWorkspace loads zero projects and reports "the language 'C#' is not supported" as an
      ordinary workspace diagnostic rather than an error. A host that only counted sentinel hits
      would have read that as "no generators executed" — a clean bill of health from an instrument
      that never ran (defect class DC-009).
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      A finding, recorded here because it is Phase-2 supply-chain evidence rather than spike
      housekeeping: Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces.MSBuild 4.14.0 transitively pulls
      Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core 17.7.2, which carries a known HIGH-severity advisory
      (GHSA-h4j7-5rxr-p4wc). The repo's TreatWarningsAsErrors turned NU1903 into a build failure,
      which is the gate working — this is the class of thing P1-SUPPLY exists to catch, and it
      surfaced on the very first Phase-2 dependency.

      Pinned forward at compile time only. At runtime the real MSBuild assemblies come from the SDK
      via MSBuildLocator (hence ExcludeAssets="runtime" throughout), so the package version governs
      what we compile against, not what executes.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
